{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975793,"nonce":"Ir4ztId+i/EGXD2Y9JNiApKo3VQ=","title":"Cross-tier FMA policy: fuse by construction so every engine agrees","message":"Every reference implementation fuses multiply-add: clang's default\n`-ffp-contract=on`, hxcpp and hxjava all produce the fused checksum for\nMandelbrot; the unfused value appears nowhere but a strict interpreter that\nrounds every opcode.\n\nash currently produces SIX distinct fused checksums across its engines, because\neach tier decides fusion by a different mechanism: the AIR `Fma` peephole,\nLLVM's own contraction under `contract` FMF, and where a frame happens to\ntransfer between tiers. `benchmarks.toml` records the checksum's LABEL rather\nthan just checking it, so a row says which fusion pattern produced the answer.\n\nCranelift has an `fma` instruction (bit-exact with `f64::mul_add`) but does not\nauto-contract, so a non-contracting tier must emit `fma` explicitly.\n\nThe fix is fusion by construction in EVERY engine, the interpreter included\n(`Fma` executed as `f64::mul_add`), driven by the AIR peephole so all engines\nagree about WHICH pairs fuse. The legacy llvm path's `contract` FMF should then\ngo. At that point the per-case `jit_tier = \"cranelift\"` pins on the two\nMandelbrot parity rows can be removed together with the accepted-checksum set.\n\nA checksum that varies with compile latency can never satisfy an exact\nexpectation, which is why those pins exist today.","files":null}]}